-    <h1>The Hachi evaluation opening, seam by seam</h1>
-    <p class="hero-sub">
-      Hachi <span class="mono">[NOZ26]</span> is a lattice-based multilinear polynomial commitment over the
-      power-of-two cyclotomic ring <span class="mono">Z_q[X]/(X^{2^α}+1)</span>. The core of one
-      opening is a <strong>single line of nine reductions</strong> — from the quadratic-form
-      evaluation (<strong>QuadEval</strong>) to a bare multilinear-evaluation claim on the committed
-      table — followed by three §4.5 adapters (rows 10–12) that hand that claim to the next
-      iteration. This map places every protocol-bearing file on that line, marks the bridges and
-      subfolders, and shows exactly what is proven, repaired, in progress — or blocked on an
-      <a href="#gap">open soundness gap in the paper's own argument</a>. The soundness side is closed by the proven
-      <strong>end-piece</strong> (send the reduced witness, <span class="mono">EndPiece/</span>):
-      <span class="mono">evaluation</span> = iteration &#8846; end-piece in
-      <span class="mono">Composition.lean</span> mirrors the completeness layer's terminal
-      reveal-and-check, and both run the same decision procedure
-      <span class="mono">endPieceCheck</span>.
-    </p>
-    <div class="hero-meta">
-      <span class="chip">45 files</span>
-      <span class="chip">36 with protocol content</span>
-      <span class="chip">9-link opening + end-piece + 3 recursion adapters</span>
-      <span class="chip">rows 1–9 + end-piece no sorried certificate · all axiom-clean</span>
-      <span class="chip">evaluation computable end to end</span>
-      <span class="chip">nonrecursive full-reveal opening, per-link complete · composition modulo <span class="mono">append_completeness</span></span>
-      <span class="chip">13 open sorries</span>
