-  <!-- ===================== THE SPINE ===================== -->
-  <section id="chain">
-    <div class="sec-head">
-      <span class="eyebrow">The composed opening</span>
-      <h2>The reduction line — QuadEval → evaluation claim</h2>
-      <p>Top to bottom is one opening of an <span class="mono">Rq</span>-committed multilinear polynomial, exactly as
-        composed in <span class="mono">Composition.lean</span> (<span class="mono">iteration</span>). Each link reduces
-        one relation to the next, and every relation is the ordinary protocol relation. Where extraction can fail
-        cryptographically instead of producing a witness (QuadEval's Module-SIS break, the weak-binding collisions
-        of Figures 4–6), the link carries an <strong>escape event</strong> on the transcript tree
-        (<span class="mono">EscapeCWSSPackage.esc</span>): a predicate on
-        <span class="mono">(statement, tree)</span> entering the certificate as a disjunct of its conclusion, so no
-        extractor can fabricate it. The final link outputs a bare multilinear-evaluation claim
-        <span class="mono">mle[w̃](a) = y′</span> on the committed table.</p>
-    </div>
-    <div id="spine"></div>
-  </section>
-
-  <hr class="rule"/>
-
-  <!-- ===================== THE GAP, EXPLAINED ===================== -->
-  <section id="gap">
-    <div class="sec-head">
-      <span class="eyebrow">The open gap, explained</span>
-      <h2>Why the recursion is on hold — the §4.5 partial-evaluation gap</h2>
-      <p>Row 9 ends the proven line with a single claim on the committed table:
-        <span class="mono">mle[w̃](a) = y′</span>, where <span class="mono">a</span> is the vector of random sumcheck
-        challenges (in the extension field <span class="mono">F ≅ F_{q^k}</span>) and <span class="mono">y′</span> is
-        pinned by the transcript — everything the iteration proved funnels into that one claim. The
-        <b>nonrecursive</b> opening simply reveals <span class="mono">w̃</span> and checks it; that path is proven
-        (<span class="mono">Correctness.lean</span>, <span class="mono">Concrete.lean</span>). Recursion (§4.5)
-        instead replaces the reveal with a proof by a smaller instance, in three moves — and the middle move
-        appears not to be knowledge-sound as the paper states it.</p>
-    </div>
-
-    <div class="grid cols3">
-      <div class="sub-card">
-        <div class="sc-top"><div><div class="sc-name">① Peel — the partial evaluations</div><div class="sc-ref">§4.5 · Eq. 24 · row 10</div></div><span class="pill wip">wip</span></div>
-        <div class="sc-desc">Split the point <span class="mono">a = (a₀, a₁)</span> with <span class="mono">a₁</span>
-          the last κ coordinates, <span class="mono">k = 2^κ</span> the extension degree. The claim factors as
-          <span class="mono">y′ = ∑ᵢ eq(i,a₁)·yᵢ</span> over the <b>partial evaluations</b>
-          <span class="mono">yᵢ := ∑ⱼ w̃_{j‖i}·eq(j,a₀)</span>, which the prover sends (the verifier derives one).
-          Residual claims: each <span class="mono">yᵢ</span> matches the <em>committed</em> table. As a reduction,
-          this seam is sound with zero error.</div>
-      </div>
-      <div class="sub-card" style="border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--bad) 40%,var(--line))">
-        <div class="sc-top"><div><div class="sc-name">② Pack — one claim instead of k</div><div class="sc-ref">§4.5 · Eqs. 25–26 · row 11</div></div><span class="pill gap">gap</span></div>
-        <div class="sc-desc">Merge the k sub-tables into one <span class="mono">F</span>-entried table
-          <span class="mono">ŵⱼ := ∑ᵢ w̃_{j‖i}·Z^{⟨i⟩}</span> along the power basis of
-          <span class="mono">F/F_q</span>, and replace the k residual claims by the <b>single</b> packed claim
-          <span class="mono">∑ⱼ ŵⱼ·eq(j,a₀) = ∑ᵢ yᵢ·Z^{⟨i⟩}</span>, whose right side is public. This one equation
-          is all the next iteration ever enforces — and it is strictly weaker than the k claims it stands for.</div>
-      </div>
-      <div class="sub-card">
-        <div class="sc-top"><div><div class="sc-name">③ Hand off — into the next ring</div><div class="sc-ref">§4.5 · Eqs. 27–28 · row 12</div></div><span class="pill wip">wip</span></div>
-        <div class="sc-desc">Convert the packed <span class="mono">F</span>-claim into the next iteration's
-          <span class="mono">R′q</span>-quadratic statement through ψ and the Theorem-2 trace identity, re-entering
-          at QuadEval over Φ′ — with no new commitment: <span class="mono">t</span> is re-read at ring dimension d′
-          through the ψ/Z-packing bijection. This seam is pinned exactly by the trace, no slack; its open items are
-          norm-parameter design (an inverse-ψ bound, linked regimes), not the soundness of the step itself.</div>
-      </div>
-    </div>
-
-    <div class="howto" style="margin-top:24px">
-      <div class="legend-card">
-        <h3>What the paper's step relies on</h3>
-        <div class="legend-row"><span class="txt">Below Eq. (26) the packing is read as an <b>equivalence</b>: proving the single packed claim should be the same as proving all k partial-evaluation claims.</span></div>
-        <div class="legend-row"><span class="txt">The forward direction is a substitution — swap two finite sums — and holds for the honest prover, so completeness is untouched.</span></div>
-        <div class="legend-row"><span class="txt">The reverse direction leans on a basis intuition: <span class="mono">1, Z, …, Z^{k−1}</span> is a basis of <span class="mono">F</span> over <span class="mono">F_q</span>, so a vanishing combination should force every coefficient to vanish. That is <em>true</em> — and it is exactly why the packing is lossless for the <b>table entries</b>, which genuinely live in <span class="mono">F_q</span>.</span></div>
-      </div>
-      <div class="legend-card">
-        <h3>Why extraction fails anyway</h3>
-        <div class="legend-row"><span class="txt">Write <span class="mono">δᵢ := pᵢ − yᵢ</span> for the defects against the true partials. The packed claim says exactly <span class="mono">∑ᵢ δᵢ·Z^{⟨i⟩} = 0</span>.</span></div>
-        <div class="legend-row"><span class="txt">But the defects are <b>not</b> in <span class="mono">F_q</span>: <span class="mono">pᵢ</span> involves <span class="mono">eq(j,a₀)</span> at a random extension-field point — the sumcheck <em>must</em> run over <span class="mono">F</span> for its own soundness — so <span class="mono">δᵢ ∈ F</span>. Over <span class="mono">F</span> this is one linear equation in k unknowns: a solution space of <span class="mono">F_q</span>-dimension (k−1)·k. Multiplying a defect by Z slides it from one slot into the next.</span></div>
-        <div class="legend-row"><span class="txt"><b>The one-line analogy:</b> base-10 <em>digits</em> make a representation unique because each digit is &lt;&nbsp;10; sums of multiples of powers of 10 with <em>arbitrary</em> coefficients are not unique — 30 = 3·10 + 0 = 2·10 + 10. The packing quietly switches from the first situation (entries) to the second (evaluations).</span></div>
-      </div>
-    </div>
-
-    <div class="cheat">
-      <div class="cheat-cap"><b>The explicit cheat at κ = 1 (k = 2)</b><span class="src">Recursion/ZBatchBridge.lean · the docstring's counterexample, in eq-weights</span></div>
-      <pre>
-<span class="cm">honest partials  p₀, p₁ ∈ F   ·   peeled challenge  a ∈ F   ·   any desired offset  Δ ≠ 0</span>
-
-prover sends     <b>y₀ := p₀ + Z·δ</b>     <b>y₁ := p₁ − δ</b>       with  δ := Δ / ((1−a)·Z − a)
-
-packed check     y₀ + Z·y₁  =  p₀ + Z·p₁               <span class="okm">✓ passes</span>  <span class="cm">— the Z·δ terms cancel; row 12 and the
-                                                                   whole next iteration are satisfied</span>
-reconstructed    (1−a)·y₀ + a·y₁  =  truth + Δ         <span class="badm">✗ cheats</span>  <span class="cm">— the pinned y′ is met for a FALSE
-                                                                   evaluation of the committed table</span>
-
-<span class="cm">timing: the yᵢ are sent after every sumcheck challenge, so a is known when δ is chosen (Fiat–Shamir
-preserves the order) · fails only at a = Z/(1+Z), one challenge value in q^k · at the paper's concrete
-parameters (k = 4), two of the four yᵢ stay completely free even after both checks</span>
-</pre>
-    </div>
-
-    <div class="grid" style="margin-top:14px">
-      <div class="sub-card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--bad)">
-        <div class="sc-top"><div><div class="sc-name">What a cheating prover gains</div><div class="sc-ref">knowledge soundness of the recursive opening</div></div></div>
-        <div class="sc-desc">Commit to any short table, run rows 1–9 for a <em>false</em> statement, and absorb the
-          entire mismatch inside the k partial evaluations. Everything proven upstream — weak binding, the corrected
-          Lemma&nbsp;10, Lemma&nbsp;11, the exact range proof — funnels through this one seam, and the seam leaks:
-          none of it transports to the recursive opening. The same pattern with monomial weights
-          <span class="mono">xⁱ</span> in place of eq-weights is §3.2's optimized <span class="mono">F_q</span> path
-          (Eq. 11, Fig. 2 row 2), so the gap is <b>not recursion-specific</b>.</div>
-      </div>
-      <div class="sub-card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ok)">
-        <div class="sc-top"><div><div class="sc-name">What stays sound</div><div class="sc-ref">rows 1–9 · completeness · §3.1</div></div></div>
-        <div class="sc-desc">The <b>nonrecursive opening</b> (rows 1–9 plus the reveal —
-          <span class="mono">Correctness.lean</span>, <span class="mono">Concrete.lean</span>) never forms the yᵢ:
-          the table itself is revealed, so Lemma&nbsp;11 applies literally. <b>Completeness</b> is untouched
-          everywhere — the honest yᵢ are the true partials. The <b>generic §3.1 transformation</b> (Fig. 2 row 1) is
-          unaffected: it introduces no prover-chosen intermediate values and pins the whole packed evaluation
-          <span class="mono">Y ∈ Rq</span> coefficient-by-coefficient. The paper's benchmarks are first-round-only
-          and never exercise the step.</div>
-      </div>
-    </div>
-
-    <div class="legend-card" style="margin-top:14px">
-      <h3>Root cause &amp; the two known repairs</h3>
-      <div class="legend-row"><span class="txt"><b>The tension:</b> the sumcheck needs its challenges in the big field <span class="mono">F</span> for soundness; the packing's basis argument needs the packed coefficients in the small field <span class="mono">F_q</span>. §4.5 needs both at once, on the same objects. The step is one sentence in the paper (“…is <em>equivalent</em> to proving (26)”) with no lemma behind it — the honest direction is true and the basis intuition is true for entries, so the failure only shows once the extraction statement is typed precisely. That is how the formalization surfaced it.</span></div>
-      <div class="legend-row"><span class="txt"><b>Repair A — a fresh batching challenge</b> over the peeled index: sample ρ <em>after</em> the yᵢ arrive and prove the ρ-weighted combination instead of the fixed Z-weighted one; a nonzero defect vector then survives with probability ≈ k/|F| (Schwartz–Zippel). One extra round, machinery close to row 5's batching bridge.</span></div>
-      <div class="legend-row"><span class="txt"><b>Repair B — drop the optimization</b>: use the generic §3.1 packing (Fig. 2 row 1), which never forms the yᵢ, at the cost of κ extra variables and a different extraction/norm story for rows 10–12.</span></div>
-      <div class="legend-row"><span class="txt">Both deviate from the printed protocol, so the decision sits <b>upstream of any Lean work</b>: write the counterexample up as a short note for the [NOZ26] authors, adopt whichever repair they take, then unblock rows 10 and 12. Until then, row 11's pull-back <span class="mono">sorry</span> stands — deliberately — as the record of where the paper's argument stands.</span></div>
-    </div>
-  </section>
